    97	## Detailed Evaluation Against the 10 Book Rules
    98	
    99	### 1. Never False
   100	* **Enacted Law vs. Proposals:** All cited laws are enacted EU regulations. Regulation (EU) 2024/2847 (CRA) was adopted 23 Oct 2024 (published 20 Nov 2024); Regulation (EU) 2023/2854 (Data Act) was adopted 13 Dec 2023.
   101	* **Deadlines & Timetables:**
   102	  * CRA reporting (Article 14) applies from 11 September 2026; main obligations apply from 11 December 2027 (Article 71(2)). Both dates are verified and accurate.
   103	  * ENISA Single Reporting Platform (SRP) live reporting date of 11 September 2026 matches ENISA documentation.
   104	  * Reporting windows (24h early warning, 72h notification, 14-day final vulnerability report after fix availability) strictly match CRA Article 14(2)–(4).
   105	  * Data Act general application date (12 September 2025) and Article 3(1) design obligation date for new products (12 September 2026) match Article 50.
   106	* **Hurdles vs. Prices:** Hypotheses are explicitly marked: *"These are required-price hypotheses, not observed market prices"* (line 50); *"The following is a model, not market evidence"* (line 56); *"No source here shows a small industrial OEM paying an external operator each month"* (line 43).
   107	* **Evaluation:** **PASSED.**
   108	
   109	### 2. Buyer and Purchase Event
   110	* **Economic Buyer:** Named as the Engineering Director or COO; Quality is the internal sponsor (line 29). The signer must have authority to assign engineering and quality hours (line 51).
   111	* **Purchase Event:** Named as a dated product release, a customer security questionnaire, or a management review exposing unowned vulnerability/data-access queues (line 29).
   112	* **Regulation vs. Demand:** The essay rigorously distinguishes statutory duty from customer budget: *"The law creates a queue, not a customer"* (line 25); *"Regulation proves workload, not paid demand"* (line 43). It cites the Risto et al. 2024/2025 survey showing that 50% of surveyed OEMs expect <20% cost impact and 25% expect zero cost impact, warning that manufacturers default to absorbing tasks internally.
   113	* **Evaluation:** **PASSED.**
   114	
   115	### 3. Economics
   116	* **Separation of Accounts:** Revenue, pass-through hardware/labs (€0), delivery costs, gross margin, operating overhead, founder replacement cost, and operating surplus are cleanly segregated in both Year 1 and mature models.
   117	* **Owner Target & Replacement Cost:** Founder replacement cost is modelled at €85k (Year 1) and €100k (Mature). The mature model yields €100k founder replacement labour + €310k operating surplus, passing the €100k compensation gate.
   118	* **Internal Arithmetic:**
   119	  * Year 1: €45k (diagnostics) + €54k (retainers) = €99k revenue. Gross margin = €72k (73%). Surplus = €72k − €15k − €85k = −€28k.
   120	  * Mature: €120k (diagnostics) + €720k (retainers) = €840k revenue. Gross margin = €540k (64%). Surplus = €540k − €130k − €100k = +€310k.
   121	  * Recurring revenue is €720k / €840k = 85.7% (~86%).
   122	* **Working Capital:** Paragraph 72 explicitly calculates a ~€90k payroll reserve required before receivables catch up for 3 delivery engineers, enforcing 50% advance billing on diagnostics and monthly advance billing on retainers to stay within the €100k investor capital limit.
   123	* **Evaluation:** **PASSED.**
